-- Darkberry Fen for Neovim. Soft dark. The background (#4c3342) is within a shade of the paint colour.
--
-- Generated by build.mjs from src/ports/neovim.lua. Do not edit ports/neovim/.
-- Install with:  cp darkberry-fen.lua ~/.config/nvim/colors/
-- ($XDG_CONFIG_HOME/nvim/colors/ on Linux and macOS, ~/AppData/Local/nvim/colors/ on
-- Windows; the same path inside a Flatpak or Snap Neovim, which read the host config).
-- Then  :colorscheme darkberry-fen  or, in init.lua,  vim.cmd.colorscheme("darkberry-fen").
-- Keep the file name: Neovim takes the scheme name from it and vim.g.colors_name below
-- has to match. Nothing to rename, no version to bump; a re-copy is a re-import.
--
-- Needs Neovim 0.9 or newer: vim.api.nvim_set_hl with `sp`, `undercurl` and `link`
-- (0.7), the Added/Changed/Removed groups and the @lsp.type.* captures (0.9). Needs
-- `termguicolors` on before the scheme loads (vim.o.termguicolors = true): the file
-- carries hex only, no cterm fallback, so on a 16- or 256-colour terminal without it
-- Neovim keeps its defaults. Every group is set with default = false, so a plugin
-- that defines its own highlights after this file wins, as it should.
--
-- Deliberately left unset: MsgArea (setting it breaks the cmdline redraw, neovim#17832);
-- NormalNC keeps the Normal background rather than dimming inactive windows, because
-- a dimmed pane would need a surface no role names; plugin groups (Telescope, nvim-cmp,
-- gitsigns, which-key) are not painted, since most of them link to the core groups below
-- and the rest would be palette names in a template; the cterm side of every group.
--
-- Host-owned pairings: none the file cannot set. Two fills are painted under syntax
-- text that keeps its own colour, as the roles intend: Visual (ui.selection, which
-- declares carriesCode 0.65) and Search (ui.search.matches, carriesCode 0.65); the
-- build measures both. DiffAdd, DiffChange, DiffDelete and DiffText carry code the
-- same way (ui.diff.*, carriesCode 0.65).
--
-- Differs from Catppuccin:
--   Visual is ui.selection alone, not surface1 with bold: bold under a selection changes
--   the glyphs, and the selection role already reads.
--   LineNr is ui.text.subtle, not surface1 (1.8:1 on Mocha's base; the guide asks for
--   line numbers people can read), and CursorLineNr is the accent, not lavender.
--   The gutter (LineNr, SignColumn, FoldColumn and their CursorLine* siblings) sits on
--   ui.pane.secondary, as Kate's IconBorder and micro's line-number do; Catppuccin
--   leaves it on base.
--   WinSeparator is ui.border.inactive (overlay1), not crust: a split edge has to be
--   seen at 3:1, and crust on base is 1.1:1.
--   PmenuSel is the fill under ui.on.fill (a row selection), not surface0 with bold;
--   PmenuMatch is ui.emphasis, not bold text.
--   IncSearch and CurSearch are ui.mark1 under ui.mark.text, not sky and red; Search is
--   the mark faded into the background, not sky at 30%.
--   TabLineSel is ui.tab.active with an underline in ui.tab.indicator, since a status
--   line cannot draw a tab's top border any other way; Catppuccin links it to Normal.
--   ErrorMsg is ui.on.error on ui.error, as micro's error bar, not red text.
--   Directory is `tint`, the accent as readable text, not blue.
--   Title and PmenuMatch share ui.emphasis (cherry); Catppuccin uses blue and bold.
--   Boolean, StorageClass and Structure follow the VS Code port (constant.language and
--   storage are keywords there), not peach and yellow.
--   PreProc, Include, Define, Macro and PreCondit are syntax.namespace, as Kate's
--   Preprocessor and Import and micro's preproc, not pink and mauve.
--   SpecialChar and @string.escape are syntax.number (VS Code: constant.character.escape),
--   not pink; Tag is syntax.link (a jump target), not lavender bold.
--   Todo is ui.warning bold on nothing, as micro's todo, not base on a flamingo fill.
--   Folded is ui.text.muted on ui.selection, the slot Kate's CodeFolding uses, not blue
--   on surface1.

vim.o.background = "dark"
vim.cmd.highlight("clear")
if vim.fn.exists("syntax_on") == 1 then vim.cmd.syntax("reset") end
vim.g.colors_name = "darkberry-fen"

